        {
          "id": 1920670,
          "username": "Lauren_Scott",
          "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/community.shopify.com/lauren_scott/{size}/417640_2.png",
          "created_at": "2026-10-22T08:52:17.728Z",
          "cooked": "<p>I’d split the checks by cost and noise. The deterministic scan is fast and repeatable, so running it on every branch in CI makes sense. A secret or an end-of-life API version is much cheaper to fix on a feature branch than after a submission has been rejected.</p>\n<p>The agent-assisted review is slower and less predictable, so I’d keep it on release branches or in a separate pre-release job rather than on every push. The reviewer’s output should end in a decision for each finding: fix it, accept it with a written reason, or mark it as a false positive with the reason written down. Any finding without a recorded decision should block the release.</p>\n<p>For the output folder, I wouldn’t commit the raw results, since they change on every run and may include details you don’t want in git history. Upload them as CI artifacts with a retention period, and commit only a short triage file listing accepted exceptions. The next run can then be compared against those decisions. I can’t confirm the exact flags for setting the output path, so check the CLI help for that.</p>",
          "post_number": 2,
          "post_type": 1,
          "posts_count": 4,
          "updated_at": "2026-10-22T08:52:17.728Z",
          "reply_count": 0,
