        {
          "id": 1921132,
          "username": "Shawn-Z",
          "avatar_template": "https://avatars.discourse-cdn.com/v4/letter/s/5f8ce5/{size}.png",
          "created_at": "2026-10-23T05:21:28.409Z",
          "cooked": "<p>I’d run the deterministic scan on every PR and gate on the high findings. Something like <code>shopify app security check</code> followed by <code>shopify app security review --blocking high</code> in CI, so the build fails on high and you still see medium and low in the log without blocking merges. Add <code>--include-dir ../backend</code> if the API lives in a separate repo, otherwise the scan won’t see those routes at all. If the file list looks wrong, <code>--list-files</code> shows what it’s actually scanning, which saves a lot of guessing.</p>\n<p>Agent review I’d keep for release branches and any PR touching auth, webhooks, or data access, not every commit.</p>\n<p>For triage, record a decision on every finding: fixed, accepted with a reason, or false positive with a reason. Keep the raw <code>.shopify/app-security/</code> output as a CI artifact, don’t commit it. Commit a short triage file instead so the next person knows why something was left alone. <code>shopify app security record</code> reads agent findings from stdin and replaces the earlier ones.</p>",
