    "ai_summary": {
      "id": 1850614,
      "summarized_text": "The recommended workflow is to run Shopify’s fast, deterministic security scan on every pull request, catching issues such as committed secrets and end-of-life API versions early. One suggested setup gates merges on high-severity findings while displaying medium and low findings without blocking; `--list-files` can confirm scan coverage, and `--include-dir ../backend` may be needed when API code is outside the app directory.\n\nUse the slower, agent-assisted review on release branches and on changes involving authentication, webhooks, or data access. Every finding should receive a documented decision: fix it, accept it with a reason, or mark it as a false positive; unresolved findings should block release. Keep raw `.shopify/app-security/` reports as CI artifacts rather than committing them, and commit a brief triage file recording accepted exceptions. The `shopify app security record` command can read agent findings from standard input and replace earlier findings.\n\nThese are proposed practices rather than a confirmed, widely tested workflow; one respondent had not yet used the check in a real release process.",
